### 2. Implementing the interface

The `ConfigManager` class provides an interface for reading and updating configuration data from a file. To implement this interface, you will need to provide two functions:

* `configManager->load()`: This function should load the configuration data from the specified file and return it as a string.
* `configManager->update(newConfigData)`: This function should update the configuration data with the new data provided in `newConfigData`.

Here is an example of how you can implement these functions using the `readFile()` and `writeFile()` functions provided earlier:
```c++
#include <fstream>
#include "config_manager.h"

class FileConfigManager : public ConfigManager {
public:
    std::string load() override {
        std::ifstream file(configFilePath);
        if (!file) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Failed to open config file");
        }
        std::string contents((std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(file)), std::istreambuf_iterator<char>());
        return contents;
    }

    void update(const std::string& newConfigData) override {
        std::ofstream file(configFilePath, std::ios::trunc);
        if (!file) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Failed to open config file for writing");
        }
        file << newConfigData;
    }
};
```
### 3. Testing the implementation

Once you have implemented the `ConfigManager` interface, you will need to test it to ensure that it works as expected. You can do this by creating a new instance of your `FileConfigManager` class and using it to load and update configuration data from a file. Here is an example of how you can test the `FileConfigManager` class:
```c++
#include <gtest/gtest.h>
