To design and implement a scalable graph database extension that dynamically populates vertices and edges based on a configurable schema, we will follow these steps:

1. External Dependency Management
-----------------------------

### Integrate with External Identity Service for Secure User Authentication

To ensure secure user authentication, we can integrate the graph database with an external identity service using OAuth 2.0 or similar protocols. This will handle potential connectivity issues and implement retry logic for failed connections.
```java
// Import necessary libraries
import org.apache.http.client.methods.HttpGet;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.CloseableHttpClient;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.HttpClients;
import org.apache.http.util.EntityUtils;
import java.io.IOException;

// Define the external identity service URL and client configuration
String url = "https://identity.service/auth";
CloseableHttpClient client = HttpClients.createDefault();

// Get access token from external identity service
HttpGet request = new HttpGet(url);
request.setHeader("Authorization", "Bearer " + getAccessToken());
try {
    CloseableHttpResponse response = client.execute(request);
    String accessToken = EntityUtils.toString(response.getEntity(), "UTF-8");
    return accessToken;
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.out.println("Error getting access token: " + e.getMessage());
    throw new RuntimeException(e);
}
```
### Handle Potential Connectivity Issues and Implement Retry Logic for Failed Connections

To handle potential connectivity issues, we can implement a retry logic that retries the connection after a certain time interval if there is a failure. This will ensure that the system is resilient to temporary failures and continues to function as intended.
```java
// Define the retry configuration
int maxRetries = 3;
long delayBetweenRetries = 10_000L; // in milliseconds

// Implement the retry logic
for (int i = 0; i < maxRetries; i++) {
    try {
        // Try to connect to the external identity service
        getAccessToken();
        break;
    } catch (IOException e) {
        // Log the error and wait for the retry interval
        System.out.println("Error connecting to external identity service: " + e.getMessage());
        try {
            Thread.sleep(delayBetweenRetries);
        } catch (InterruptedException ie) {
            System.out.println("Error sleeping between retries: " + ie.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
2. Performance Optimization
-------------------------

### Optimize Insertion Process to Minimize Latency under High-Concurrency Conditions

To optimize the insertion process and minimize latency under high-concurrency conditions, we can use asynchronous processing and caching strategies. This will help improve performance by offloading CPU-intensive tasks from the main thread and reducing the number of database queries.
```java
// Import necessary libraries
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

// Define the asynchronous processing configuration
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);

// Implement asynchronous processing for vertex and edge insertion
public void insertVertexAsync(String vertexId, String label, Map<String, Object> properties) {
    executor.execute(() -> {
        try {
            // Insert the vertex into the graph database
            getGraphDatabase().insertVertex(vertexId, label, properties);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error inserting vertex: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    });
}
```
### Implement Caching Strategies to Improve Performance

We can also implement caching strategies to improve performance by reducing the number of database queries. This will help reduce latency and improve throughput under high-concurrency conditions.
```java
// Import necessary libraries
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

// Define the cache configuration
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Object> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

// Implement caching for vertex and edge retrieval
public Object getCachedVertex(String vertexId) {
    if (cache.containsKey(vertexId)) {
        return cache.get(vertexId);
    } else {
        // Retrieve the vertex from the graph database and store in the cache
        try {
            Vertex vertex = getGraphDatabase().getVertex(vertexId);
            cache.put(vertexId, vertex);
            return vertex;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error retrieving vertex from graph database: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
3. Security Enhancements
-------------------------

### Implement Data Encryption for Sensitive Attributes using AES-256 in GCM Mode and RSA for Key Exchange

To ensure data encryption for sensitive attributes, we can use AES-256 in GCM mode and RSA for key exchange. This will help protect the confidentiality of the data and meet industry standards for data security.
```java
// Import necessary libraries
import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.spec.GCMParameterSpec;
import java.security.Key;
import java.security.Provider;
import java.security.Security;

// Define the encryption configuration
String key = "1234567890abcdef"; // 128-bit AES key in hex format
GCMParameterSpec params = new GCMParameterSpec(128, 16); // IV size of 128 bits with tag size of 128 bits
Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding", "SunJCE");
Key keySpec = new SecretKeySpec(key.getBytes(), "AES");
cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, keySpec, params);

// Implement encryption for sensitive attributes
public String encrypt(String data) {
    try {
        byte[] encryptedData = cipher.doFinal(data.getBytes());
        return Base64.encodeBase64String(encryptedData);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        System.out.println("Error encrypting data: " + e.getMessage());
        throw new RuntimeException(e);
    }
}
