void log_error(const char *message, ...) {
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, message);
    int len = vsnprintf(NULL, 0, message, args) + 1;
    char *buf = malloc(len);
    vsnprintf(buf, len, message, args);
    va_end(args);
    fputs(buf, stderr);
    free(buf);
}
```
This function takes a variable number of arguments and uses `va_start`, `vsnprintf`, and `va_end` to format the message into a buffer. It then logs the buffer to the error log using `fputs`. The function is designed to be flexible in terms of the number of arguments it can take, and it uses `malloc` to dynamically allocate memory for the formatted message.

The function also uses `stderr` as the file descriptor for logging errors, which means that any output generated by this function will appear on the console or terminal where the program is running. If you want to log errors to a different file or stream, you can modify the function accordingly.']

