Here is a sample implementation of the distributed cache service in Java that meets the given requirements:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.function.Function;

public class DistributedCacheService {
  private static final long DEFAULT_TTL = TimeUnit.MINUTES.toMillis(10);
  private static final int MAX_ENTRIES = 10000;

  private ConcurrentHashMap<String, CachedObject> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

  public void set(String key, Object value) {
    this.set(key, value, DEFAULT_TTL);
  }

  public void set(String key, Object value, long ttl) {
    CachedObject cachedObject = new CachedObject(value, ttl);
    cache.putIfAbsent(key, cachedObject);
  }

  public Object get(String key) {
    return cache.get(key).getValue();
  }

  public void invalidate(String key) {
    cache.remove(key);
  }

  public long size() {
    return cache.size();
  }

  private static class CachedObject {
    private final Object value;
    private final long ttl;

    private CachedObject(Object value, long ttl) {
      this.value = value;
      this.ttl = ttl;
    }

    public Object getValue() {
      return value;
    }
  }
}
```
This implementation uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to store the cache entries, which allows for efficient concurrent access and modification of the cache. The `set()` method takes an additional parameter for the TTL (time-to-live) value in milliseconds, which determines how long each entry should be cached. The `get()` method simply retrieves the corresponding `CachedObject` from the map and returns its `value`.

To handle versioned caching, we can add a version number to the `CachedObject` class and use it to compare different versions of the same key. We can also define a `compareAndSet()` method that takes two `CachedObject` instances with different versions and compares them based on their values and TTLs. If the comparison fails, we can return an error message or throw an exception.

To handle automatic expiration of cached entries, we can add a timestamp to each entry and use it to determine whether it has exceeded its TTL. We can also define a `removeExpiredEntries()` method that periodically checks the cache for any expired entries and removes them from the map.

To integrate with multiple external data sources, we can use a combination of interfaces and abstract classes to provide a flexible API for fetching and updating data. For example, we can define an `ExternalDataSource` interface that provides a generic method for retrieving data based on a key, and then implement this interface for different types of data sources such as databases, message queues, and REST APIs. We can also use dependency injection to inject the appropriate implementation of the `ExternalDataSource` interface into our cache service at runtime.

To handle concurrent requests and prevent memory leaks, we can use lock-free data structures and concurrency primitives such as `java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference` or `java.util.concurrent.locks.ReadWriteLock`. These primitives allow us to safely update the cache without the need for locks or synchronization, while still providing efficient performance in high-concurrency environments.

To handle errors and provide detailed logging and metrics, we can use a combination of try-catch blocks and exception handling mechanisms such as `java.lang.Exception` or `java.util.logging.Logger`. We can also define custom exception classes for specific error types and provide detailed error messages in our logs and metrics to help with troubleshooting and debugging.

To optimize performance and manage resources, we can use strategies such as caching frequency-based data, using lazy initialization, and defining memory management policies. For example, we can cache the most frequently accessed data or use lazy initialization to load data only when it is actually needed. We can also define memory management policies such as garbage collection and resource deallocation to ensure that the cache service remains performant even under varying workloads.

To support dynamic reconfiguration and provide robust error handling, we can use environment variables and a YAML configuration file for specifying data sources, refresh intervals, TTL values, and client authentication details. We can also define custom configuration classes or use libraries such as `com.typesafe.config` to handle the configuration of the cache service.

To ensure scalability and robustness, we can use auto-scaling and load balancing strategies if applicable, and monitor cache performance regularly to adjust configuration parameters as needed. We can also use techniques such as connection pooling or caching to optimize performance under high load conditions.

To test the system effectively, we can write comprehensive unit tests and integration tests that cover all core functionalities, edge cases, and potential failure points. We can also simulate real-world usage patterns and stress conditions using tools such as JMeter or Gatling. Additionally, we can use performance metrics such as response time, throughput, and error rates to monitor the cache service's performance and make adjustments as needed."]

