        sched_yield();
    }
    return false;
}

void release_semaphore(Semaphore* sem) {
    // Release the semaphore and mark it as unlocked
    sem->locked = false;
}
```
10. **Distributed System**: Implement a distributed system that uses the semaphore to coordinate between nodes in the system.
```c
void acquire_semaphore(Semaphore* sem) {
    // If the semaphore is currently unlocked, acquire it and return true
    if (!sem->locked) {
        sem->locked = true;
        return true;
    }
    // Otherwise, wait for the semaphore to be released before returning false
    while (sem->locked) {
        // Yield the CPU to allow other threads to execute
        sched_yield();
    }
    return false;
}

void release_semaphore(Semaphore* sem) {
    // Release the semaphore and mark it as unlocked
    sem->locked = false;
}
```
It is important to note that this is just a basic example of how a semaphore can be used, and there are many other ways it can be implemented. Additionally, the examples provided above are in C, but the concepts and ideas can be applied to any programming language that supports threads.']

