1. Modular Pipeline for Parsing and Validating JSON Payloads:

The pipeline will be designed as a series of modular stages that can be easily composed and reconfigured as needed. Each stage will be responsible for a specific task, such as parsing JSON data or validating against a schema. The pipeline will use a combination of static and dynamic type checking mechanisms to ensure data integrity and system reliability.
```c++
#include <json/json.h> // JSON library
#include <type_traits> // Type traits for dynamic typing

class JsonPipeline {
public:
    void parseAndValidate(const std::string& json) {
        Json::Value root;
        Json::Reader reader;
        if (!reader.parse(json, root)) {
            throw JsonException("Invalid JSON");
        }

        for (auto stage : stages_) {
            stage->process(root);
        }
    }

private:
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<JsonPipelineStage>> stages_;
};
```
2. Asynchronous Event Processing Engine:

The engine will use a custom thread pool to optimize resource usage and minimize context switching. The thread pool will be responsible for scheduling and executing tasks as needed, while the event processing stage will handle the actual processing of events.
```c++
#include <thread> // Thread library
#include <mutex> // Mutex for synchronization
#include <condition_variable> // Condition variable for waiting on threads

class EventProcessingEngine {
public:
    void processEvents(const std::vector<Event>& events) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        threadPool_.emplace_back([this, events]() {
            for (auto& event : events) {
                this->processEvent(event);
            }
        });
    }

private:
    std::vector<std::thread> threadPool_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
};
```
3. Caching Mechanism:

The caching mechanism will use a fixed-size LRU cache to store frequently accessed data. The cache will support configurable eviction policies and be resilient to memory pressure, allowing for graceful degradation when resources are constrained.
```c++
#include <unordered_map> // Unordered map for caching
#include <list> // List for LRU ordering
#include <mutex> // Mutex for synchronization
#include <condition_variable> // Condition variable for waiting on cache

class Cache {
public:
    void add(const std::string& key, const std::string& value) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        auto iter = cache_.find(key);
        if (iter != cache_.end()) {
            // Update existing entry
            iter->second = value;
        } else {
            // Add new entry
            cache_.emplace(std::make_pair(key, value));
        }
    }

    void get(const std::string& key) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        auto iter = cache_.find(key);
        if (iter != cache_.end()) {
            // Return cached value
            return iter->second;
        } else {
            // Cache miss
            return nullptr;
        }
    }

private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> cache_;
    std::list<std::string> lruList_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
};
```
4. Memory Usage Optimization:

To optimize memory usage, the application will leverage modern C++ features like smart pointers and RAII, while also considering the use of external libraries for performance-critical sections. The trade-offs between using standard library containers and third-party alternatives for specific use cases should be carefully evaluated.
```c++
#include <memory> // Smart pointer library
#include <unordered_map> // Unordered map for caching
#include <list> // List for LRU ordering
#include <vector> // Vector for storing events

class MemoryOptimization {
public:
    void optimizeMemoryUsage(const std::vector<Event>& events) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        auto& cache = getCache();
        for (auto& event : events) {
            // Use smart pointers to manage memory
            cache.add(event.getKey(), std::make_unique<Event>(event));
        }
    }

private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<Event>> cache_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
};
```
5. Robust Error-Handling System:

The error-handling system will use a combination of logging and monitoring tools to provide real-time alerts and metrics, enabling proactive maintenance and automated recovery where possible. The system should also integrate with external services for fault tolerance and disaster recovery.
```c++
#include <iostream> // Standard input/output library
#include <fstream> // File stream for logging
#include <chrono> // Time measurement library
#include <mutex> // Mutex for synchronization
#include <condition_variable> // Condition variable for waiting on cache

class ErrorHandlingSystem {
public:
    void handleError(const std::exception& e) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        auto now = std::chrono::system_clock::now();
        // Log error to file stream
        logStream_ << "[" << now.time_since_epoch().count() << "] Error: " << e.what() << std::endl;
    }

private:
    std::ofstream logStream_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
};
```
6. Scalability and Performance:

To ensure scalability and high performance, the application will use a distributed architecture with multiple servers, load balancers, and caching mechanisms to handle large volumes of data. The system should also be designed to handle failures and recover gracefully without compromising data consistency or availability.
```c++
#include <vector> // Vector for storing events
#include <unordered_map> // Unordered map for caching
#include <list> // List for LRU ordering
#include <memory> // Smart pointer library
#include <mutex> // Mutex for synchronization
#include <condition_variable> // Condition variable for waiting on cache

class ScalableSystem {
public:
    void processEvents(const std::vector<Event>& events) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        auto& cache = getCache();
        // Distribute events among multiple servers for processing
        for (auto& event : events) {
            cache.add(event.getKey(), std::make_unique<Event>(event));
        }
    }

private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<Event>> cache_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
};
```']

