['Here is a possible implementation of the modular Rust-based tool that dynamically processes source code repositories:
```
#[derive(Debug)]
struct Repository {
    name: String,
    files: Vec<File>,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
struct File {
    path: String,
    contents: Vec<u8>,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
enum OptimizationLevel {
    Basic,
    Medium,
    Aggressive,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
enum LoggingVerbosity {
    Minimal,
    Normal,
    Verbose,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
enum OutputFormat {
    Text,
    JSON,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
struct ProcessingParameters {
    optimization_level: OptimizationLevel,
    logging_verbosity: LoggingVerbosity,
    output_format: OutputFormat,
}

fn process_repository(repo: &Repository) -> Result<Vec<u8>, String> {
    // Extract the files from the repository and convert them to LLVM IR.
    let mut llvm_ir = Vec::new();
    for file in &repo.files {
        match generate_llvm_ir(&file.contents) {
            Ok(ir) => llvm_ir.extend(ir),
            Err(e) => return Err(format!("Error generating LLVM IR: {}", e)),
        }
    }

    // Apply optimization passes to the LLVM IR.
    let optimized_ir = optimize_llvm_ir(&llvm_ir, repo.optimization_level);

    // Convert the optimized LLVM IR into the desired output format.
    match repo.output_format {
        OutputFormat::Text => Ok(text_format(&optimized_ir)),
        OutputFormat::JSON => Ok(json_format(&optimized_ir)),
    }
}

fn generate_llvm_ir(contents: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, String> {
    // Use the LLVM IR compiler to convert the source code into LLVM IR.
    let mut compile_command = "clang";
    compile_command += "-Xclang -load -Xclang libLLVMMCJIT.dylib";
    compile_command += "-emit-llvm";

    let output = Command::new("sh")
        .arg("-c")
        .arg(compile_command)
        .stdin(Stdio::piped())
        .stdout(Stdio::piped())
        .spawn()?;

    // Feed the source code into the command and retrieve the LLVM IR.
    let mut child = output.child;
    {
        let stdin = child.stdin.take().unwrap();
        write!(stdin, "{}", contents)?;
    }
    drop(child);

    // Read the LLVM IR from the command's stdout.
    let mut llvm_ir = Vec::new();
    {
        let stdout = output.stdout.take().unwrap();
        read_to_end(&mut llvm_ir, &mut stdout)?;
    }

    // Return the LLVM IR as a vector of bytes.
    Ok(llvm_ir)
}

fn optimize_llvm_ir(ir: &[u8], level: OptimizationLevel) -> Vec<u8> {
    // Use the LLVM optimizer to apply optimization passes to the LLVM IR.
    let mut opt_command = "opt";
    opt_command += "-O";
    opt_command += match level {
        OptimizationLevel::Basic => "1",
        OptimizationLevel::Medium => "2",
        OptimizationLevel::Aggressive => "3",
    };

    let output = Command::new("sh")
        .arg("-c")
        .arg(opt_command)
        .stdin(Stdio::piped())
        .stdout(Stdio::piped())
        .spawn()?;

    // Feed the LLVM IR into the command and retrieve the optimized LLVM IR.
    let mut child = output.child;
    {
        let stdin = child.stdin.take().unwrap();
        write!(stdin, "{}", ir)?;
    }
    drop(child);

    // Read the optimized LLVM IR from the command's stdout.
    let mut optimized_ir = Vec::new();
    {
        let stdout = output.stdout.take().unwrap();
        read_to_end(&mut optimized_ir, &mut stdout)?;
    }

    // Return the optimized LLVM IR as a vector of bytes.
    Ok(optimized_ir)
}

fn text_format(ir: &[u8]) -> String {
    // Convert the LLVM IR into a human-readable format.
    let mut text = String::new();
    for line in ir.lines() {
        if line.starts_with(";") {
            continue;
        }
        text += &format!("{}\n", line);
    }
    text
}

fn json_format(ir: &[u8]) -> String {
    // Convert the LLVM IR into a JSON format.
    let mut json = String::new();
    for line in ir.lines() {
        if line.starts_with(";") {
            continue;
        }
        json += &format!("{{ \"line\": \"{}\" }}\n", line);
    }
    json
}
```
This code defines the `Repository` struct, which contains the name of the repository and a vector of `File` objects. The `File` struct contains the path to the file in the repository and its contents as a vector of bytes.

The `process_repository` function takes a reference to a `Repository` object and returns a result containing either the optimized LLVM IR as a vector of bytes or an error message if there was an error processing the repository. The function first extracts the files from the repository, converts them into LLVM IR using the `generate_llvm_ir` function, applies optimization passes to the LLVM IR using the `optimize_llvm_ir` function, and then converts the optimized LLVM IR into the desired output format using the `text_format` or `json_format` functions.

The `generate_llvm_ir` function uses the `clang` command to compile the source code into LLVM IR. It takes a reference to the source code as a vector of bytes and returns a result containing either the LLVM IR as a vector of bytes or an error message if there was an error compiling the code.

The `optimize_llvm_ir` function uses the `opt` command to apply optimization passes to the LLVM IR. It takes a reference to the LLVM IR as a vector of bytes and returns a result containing either the optimized LLVM IR as a vector of bytes or an error message if there was an error optimizing the code.

The `text_format` function converts the LLVM IR into a human-readable format by stripping out comments and removing unnecessary whitespace. It takes a reference to the LLVM IR as a vector of bytes and returns the converted text as a string.

The `json_format` function converts the LLVM IR into a JSON format by wrapping each line in a JSON object. It takes a reference to the LLVM IR as a vector of bytes and returns the converted JSON as a string.']

