This implementation uses a `ScheduledExecutorService` to periodically poll for changes in the file systems. Each provider is responsible for polling its own filesystem and reporting any changes it finds. The service then updates its internal state with the latest information.

The `FileManager` class provides a simple API for adding providers and monitoring file systems. It also schedules periodic polling tasks using the `scheduleAtFixedRate()` method of the `ScheduledExecutorService`.

The `CloudStorageProvider` interface defines a method to get the ID of the filesystem that the provider is responsible for, as well as a method to poll for changes in that filesystem. The `FileSystem` class implements this interface and provides an API for adding and removing files, getting a file by name, and polling for changes.

The `File` class represents a single file in the file system and has methods to get its name and content.

To handle large-scale directories, this implementation uses a hash map to keep track of the files in each filesystem. This allows it to efficiently store and retrieve information about the files in the file systems.

The service is designed to be idempotent, meaning that it can safely execute the same operation multiple times without causing any harm. It also handles partial failures gracefully by logging errors and continuing to operate normally.

To maximize throughput while minimizing disk I/O and CPU usage, this implementation uses a single thread executor to schedule polling tasks. This allows it to perform file operations concurrently, but ensures that no more than one task is executed at a time.

The service can be integrated with monitoring tools to alert administrators of potential issues. For example, if the service encounters an error while polling for changes in a filesystem, it can log the error and notify an administrator via email or other means.

To optimize for both local and remote environments, this implementation uses a modular design that allows new storage provider integrations to be easily added. This makes it easier to adapt to changing requirements and ensure that the service remains efficient and reliable.

Finally, detailed logging is provided to aid in troubleshooting issues. This can help administrators identify and resolve problems quickly, ensuring that the service remains available and functional.']

