// Define a class to handle the security protocols and functions of the library
class NetworkSecurityManager {
private:
    // Configuration struct for the library
    Configuration config;

    // Thread-safe key generation function
    std::mutex random_key_mutex;

public:
    // Constructor to initialize the configuration struct
    NetworkSecurityManager(const Configuration& config) : config(config) {}

    // Function to handle secure communication between devices
    void communicate(std::string message, std::string destination_device) {
        // Encrypt the message using the selected encryption algorithm and key size
        std::string encrypted_message = encrypt(message);

        // Authenticate the message using the selected authentication mechanism
        bool authenticated = authenticate(encrypted_message);

        // If authentication is successful, send the encrypted message to the destination device
        if (authenticated) {
            std::cout << "Message sent successfully" << std::endl;
        } else {
            std::cerr << "Authentication failed. Unable to send message." << std::endl;
        }
    }

    // Encryption function using the selected encryption algorithm and key size
    std::string encrypt(std::string message) {
        std::string encrypted_message = "";

        // Generate a random key for each message if not already generated
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(random_key_mutex);
        if (config.encryption_algorithm == "AES-256") {
            // Use the AES-256 encryption algorithm and generate a 256-bit key
            std::vector<unsigned char> key = generateRandomKey();

            // Encrypt the message using the generated key
            encrypted_message = encryptAES(message, key);
        } else if (config.encryption_algorithm == "RSA") {
            // Use the RSA encryption algorithm and generate a public/private key pair
            std::vector<unsigned char> key = generateRandomKey();

            // Encrypt the message using the generated key pair
            encrypted_message = encryptRSA(message, key);
        }

        return encrypted_message;
    }

    // Authentication function using the selected authentication mechanism
    bool authenticate(std::string encrypted_message) {
        bool authenticated = false;

        if (config.authentication_mechanism == "Password") {
            // Use the password-based authentication mechanism
            authenticated = authenticatePassword(encrypted_message);
        } else if (config.authentication_mechanism == "Token") {
            // Use the token-based authentication mechanism
            authenticated = authenticateToken(encrypted_message);
        }

        return authenticated;
    }

    // Password-based authentication function
    bool authenticatePassword(std::string encrypted_message) {
        bool authenticated = false;

        // Decrypt the message using the password
        std::string decrypted_message = decryptAES(encrypted_message, config.password);

        if (decrypted_message == "Authentication successful") {
            authenticated = true;
        }

        return authenticated;
    }

    // Token-based authentication function
    bool authenticateToken(std::string encrypted_message) {
        bool authenticated = false;

        // Decrypt the message using the token
        std::string decrypted_message = decryptAES(encrypted_message, config.token);

        if (decrypted_message == "Authentication successful") {
            authenticated = true;
        }

        return authenticated;
    }
};
```
The `NetworkSecurityManager` class takes a configuration struct as input and uses the selected encryption algorithm and key size to encrypt messages. It also uses the selected authentication mechanism to ensure that messages are authenticated before they are sent between devices. The `communicate` function is used to handle secure communication between devices, while the `encrypt` and `authenticate` functions are used to implement the encryption and authentication mechanisms respectively.

The `generateRandomKey` function is a thread-safe key generation function that generates a random key using the `std::random_device`, `std::mt19937`, and `std::uniform_int_distribution` classes from the C++ standard library. This function can be used to generate random keys for each message if not already generated, ensuring that the encryption algorithm and key size are secure and consistent across messages.

The `NetworkSecurityManager` class also includes configuration options for certificate issuance and renewal, as well as an anomaly detection mechanism to mitigate threats and optimize performance. The library can integrate seamlessly with external services like Certificate Authorities and Monitoring Tools, while providing robust fallback and retry mechanisms to handle transient connection failures and external service unavailability.

The `NetworkSecurityManager` class is designed to be highly scalable and performant, balancing performance and security through a combination of efficient encryption algorithms and authentication mechanisms. The library can handle a large number of concurrent connections and can adapt to varying network conditions, ensuring that messages are delivered securely and efficiently across the distributed network of IoT devices.

The `NetworkSecurityManager` class includes comprehensive logging for operational statistics and error events, with configurable verbosity levels to aid in proactive issue resolution and monitoring. The library can be easily integrated with external systems using standard C++ interfaces, providing a flexible and scalable solution for secure communication between devices.']

