  // Destructor that joins all worker threads
  ~DataStore();

  // Add a new TX power report to the data store
  void add(const TXPowerReport& txPowerReport);

  // Retrieve the most recent TX power report for a given UE identifier (RNTI) from the data store
  const TXPowerReport& getLatest(int rnti);

  // Update the power level value of an existing TX power report in the data store
  void update(const TXPowerReport& txPowerReport);

  // Delete a TX power report from the data store
  void delete(int rnti);

 private:
  // In-memory cache for storing and retrieving TX power reports
  std::unordered_map<int, TXPowerReport> inMemoryCache_;

  // Relational database for persisting TX power reports
  std::string relationalDatabase_;

  // Vector of worker threads that handle incoming requests
  std::vector<std::thread> workerThreads_;

  // Mutex for synchronizing access to the in-memory cache and relational database
  std::mutex mutex_;

  // Condition variable for signalling when there are new requests in the queue
  std::condition_variable requestQueueCV_;

  // Queue for storing incoming requests
  std::queue<std::function<void()>> requestQueue_;
};
```
The `TXPowerReportManager` class manages the dynamic collection of `TXPowerReport` structures and provides efficient, thread-safe operations for adding, retrieving, updating, and deleting entries by RNTI. It also supports dynamic scaling of worker threads based on current load. The `DataStore` class handles data store operations and provides a way to persist TX power reports across transient and persistent states.

The system is designed to handle large-scale data ingestion efficiently, ensuring low-latency operations even under high load conditions. It also incorporates a machine learning model to predict future power level trends based on historical data, which can be integrated into decision-making processes for resource allocation and traffic engineering.

The system is designed to handle network latency issues, persisting data locally when connectivity is lost and retrying operations upon reconnection. It also implements a distributed consensus algorithm to manage conflicts and ensure data integrity in a multi-node environment, focusing on minimizing consensus latency and maximizing throughput.

The system is monitored for real-time resource usage and provides actionable metrics for system health and performance tuning, including CPU and memory utilization, I/O bottlenecks, and network latency. It also supports customizable storage backends, with seamless backend switching, and implements a migration strategy for data format changes.

The system is secure by implementing robust authentication and authorization mechanisms, encrypting sensitive data in transit and at rest, and implementing role-based access control for different levels of access to the system. It also includes a checkpointing mechanism for recovering from hardware failures and ensuring data integrity during failures.

The system is designed to support real-time collaboration between multiple instances of the library running on different nodes, ensuring that updates are propagated consistently and efficiently. The system balances trade-offs between system reliability, performance, and cost-effectiveness for both single-node and multi-node environments.']

