### Using Entity Framework Core with a relational database

Entity Framework Core (EF Core) is an Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) library that allows .NET developers to work with databases using .NET objects and C# code. EF Core supports multiple database providers, including relational databases like SQL Server, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.

To use EF Core with a relational database, you need to install the corresponding provider package from NuGet:
```
Install-Package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer
```
Here's an example of how you can use EF Core with a SQL Server database:
```csharp
using System;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

namespace EfExample
{
    public class Blog
    {
        public int Id { get; set; }
        public string Name { get; set; }
        public string Url { get; set; }
    }

    public class EfDbContext : DbContext
    {
        public EfDbContext(DbContextOptions<EfDbContext> options)
            : base(options)
        {
        }

        public DbSet<Blog> Blogs { get; set; }
    }

    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            var serviceProvider = new ServiceCollection()
                .AddDbContext<EfDbContext>(options => options.UseSqlServer("Server=myserver;Database=mydatabase;User Id=myuser;Password=mypassword;"))
                .BuildServiceProvider();

            using (var dbContext = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService<EfDbContext>())
            {
                var blog = new Blog { Name = "My Blog", Url = "http://myblog.com" };
                dbContext.Blogs.Add(blog);
                dbContext.SaveChanges();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we create a `DbContext` class that inherits from the `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.DbContext` class. This class represents a session with the database and provides methods for querying and saving data.

We also define a `Blog` class that represents a blog in our application. The `Blogs` property on the `EfDbContext` class is of type `DbSet<Blog>`, which allows us to work with a collection of blogs in our database.

In the `Main` method, we create a new instance of the `ServiceCollection` class from the `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` namespace and add an instance of the `EfDbContext` class to it using the `AddDbContext<T>` method. This allows us to use dependency injection to resolve the database context as needed.

We then create a new instance of the `Blog` class and add it to the `Blogs` collection in our database context. Finally, we call the `SaveChanges` method to persist the changes to the database.

### Using Entity Framework Core with a non-relational database

Entity Framework Core (EF Core) is an Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) library that allows .NET developers to work with databases using .NET objects and C# code. EF Core supports multiple database providers, including relational databases like SQL Server, MySQL, and PostgreSQL, as well as non-relational databases like MongoDB and Cassandra.

To use EF Core with a non-relational database, you need to install the corresponding provider package from NuGet:
```
Install-Package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.MongoDb
```
Here's an example of how you can use EF Core with a MongoDB database:
```csharp
using System;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using MongoDb.Driver;

namespace EfExample
{
    public class Blog
    {
        [BsonId]
        public ObjectId Id { get; set; }
        public string Name { get; set; }
        public string Url { get; set; }
    }

    public class EfDbContext : DbContext
    {
        public EfDbContext(DbContextOptions<EfDbContext> options)
            : base(options)
        {
        }

        public DbSet<Blog> Blogs { get; set; }
    }

    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            var serviceProvider = new ServiceCollection()
                .AddDbContext<EfDbContext>(options => options.UseMongoDb("mongodb://localhost:27017", "mydatabase"))
                .BuildServiceProvider();

            using (var dbContext = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService<EfDbContext>())
            {
                var blog = new Blog { Name = "My Blog", Url = "http://myblog.com" };
                dbContext.Blogs.Add(blog);
                dbContext.SaveChanges();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we create a `DbContext` class that inherits from the `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.DbContext` class. This class represents a session with the database and provides methods for querying and saving data.

We also define a `Blog` class that represents a blog in our application. The `Blogs` property on the `EfDbContext` class is of type `DbSet<Blog>`, which allows us to work with a collection of blogs in our database.

In the `Main` method, we create a new instance of the `ServiceCollection` class from the `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` namespace and add an instance of the `EfDbContext` class to it using the `AddDbContext<T>` method. This allows us to use dependency injection to resolve the database context as needed.

We then create a new instance of the `Blog` class and add it to the `Blogs` collection in our database context. Finally, we call the `SaveChanges` method to persist the changes to the database.

### Conclusion

In this tutorial, we learned how to use Entity Framework Core (EF Core) with a non-relational database like MongoDB or Cassandra. We created a new project and added the required packages using NuGet. We then defined our `Blog` class and created an instance of the `EfDbContext` class using dependency injection. Finally, we added a new blog to the collection in our database context and saved the changes to the database using the `SaveChanges` method.']

