class AuthenticationService {
    private $oauthServer;

    public function __construct() {
        $this->oauthServer = new OAuthServer(array(
            'response_types' => array('code', 'token', 'id_token'),
            'grant_types' => array('authorization_code', 'refresh_token', 'client_credentials', 'password', 'implicit'),
        ));
    }

    public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
        return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope);
    }

    public function refreshToken($refreshToken) {
        return $this->oauthServer->issueRefreshToken($refreshToken);
    }

    public function validateToken($accessToken) {
        return $this->oauthServer->validateAccessToken($accessToken);
    }
}
```
This implementation uses the `OAuth2` library to handle OAuth 2.0 authentication and authorization. The `AuthenticationService` class provides methods for authorizing users, refreshing tokens, validating access tokens, and providing user information.

The `OAuthServer` instance is created with the required response types and grant types for the service. The `authorize()` method takes a user object and an optional scope parameter, and returns an access token in exchange for the provided credentials. The `refreshToken()` method takes a refresh token string and returns a new access token if the refresh token is valid.

The `validateToken()` method takes an access token string and verifies its validity. It returns an array containing the user information and any scope associated with the token, or null if the token is invalid.

To implement rate limiting and token expiration checks, you can use the `OAuth2` library's built-in features or implement your own custom logic. For example:
```php
public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
    // Check if user has exceeded rate limit
    if ($this->hasExceededRateLimit($user)) {
        throw new RateLimitedException();
    }

    // Check if token is expired
    if ($this->tokenIsExpired($accessToken)) {
        throw new TokenExpiredException();
    }

    // Issue access token
    return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope);
}
```
To implement refresh token mechanisms and prevent abuse, you can use the `OAuth2` library's built-in features or implement your own custom logic. For example:
```php
public function refreshToken($refreshToken) {
    // Check if refresh token is valid
    if ($this->oauthServer->validateRefreshToken($refreshToken)) {
        // Issue new access token
        return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope);
    } else {
        throw new InvalidRefreshTokenException();
    }
}
```
To support both short-lived and long-lived tokens, you can use the `OAuth2` library's built-in features or implement your own custom logic. For example:
```php
public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
    // Issue access token with specified lifetime
    if ($this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope, array('expires_in' => 3600))) {
        return true;
    } else {
        throw new TokenGenerationException();
    }
}
```
To implement comprehensive error handling and logging mechanisms, you can use the `OAuth2` library's built-in features or implement your own custom logic. For example:
```php
public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
    try {
        // Issue access token
        return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope);
    } catch (OAuthException $e) {
        // Log error
        error_log("Error generating access token: {$e->getMessage()}");
        throw new TokenGenerationException();
    }
}
```
To ensure that the service can dynamically adjust its load balancing and request throttling based on real-time server metrics and external dependency health checks, you can use third-party monitoring tools or implement your own custom logic. For example:
```php
public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
    // Check if service is under heavy load
    if ($this->isUnderHeavyLoad()) {
        throw new ServiceOverloadedException();
    }

    // Issue access token
    return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope);
}
```
To detect and recover from transient failures in downstream services, you can use circuit breakers or retry policies to automatically reroute requests to alternative servers. For example:
```php
public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
    // Check if service is available
    if ($this->isAvailable()) {
        return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope);
    } else {
        throw new ServiceUnavailableException();
    }
}
```
To optimize for memory and CPU usage, you can use the `OAuth2` library's built-in features or implement your own custom logic. For example:
```php
public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
    // Optimize for memory usage
    if ($this->optimizeForMemoryUsage()) {
        return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope);
    } else {
        throw new MemoryOptimizationException();
    }
}
```
To implement secure data storage and retrieval mechanisms, you can use the `OAuth2` library's built-in features or implement your own custom logic. For example:
```php
public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
    // Check if user has previously issued a token
    if ($this->hasIssuedToken($user)) {
        return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope);
    } else {
        throw new TokenGenerationException();
    }
}
```
To provide user information and scope information in the access token, you can use the `OAuth2` library's built-in features or implement your own custom logic. For example:
```php
public function authorize($user, $scope = null) {
    // Issue access token with user information and scope
    return $this->oauthServer->issueAccessToken($user, $scope, array('user' => $user, 'scope' => $scope));
