Here's a sample implementation of the proposed spatial indexing system in C++ using an octree data structure and an external distributed database:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <tuple>
#include <chrono>
#include <memory>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

// Define the external distributed database interface
class ExternalDatabase {
 public:
  // Insert a new data point into the database
  virtual void insert(const std::vector<float>& coordinates) = 0;

  // Delete a data point from the database
  virtual void delete(const std::vector<float>& coordinates) = 0;

  // Update an existing data point in the database
  virtual void update(const std::vector<float>& coordinates) = 0;

  // Perform a batch operation on multiple data points
  virtual void batch_operation(std::vector<std::tuple<int, int, float>>& operations) = 0;

  // Query the database for nearby data points to a given point
  virtual std::vector<std::pair<float, std::vector<float>>> query_nearby(const std::vector<float>& coordinates, int k) = 0;

  // Query the database for all data points within a given range
  virtual std::set<std::vector<float>> query_range(const std::vector<float>& coordinates, float radius) = 0;
};

// Define an octree data structure to store the spatial index
class Octree {
 public:
  // Constructor
  Octree(int grid_size, int leaf_node_capacity);

  // Destructor
  ~Octree();

  // Insert a new data point into the tree
  void insert(const std::vector<float>& coordinates);

  // Delete a data point from the tree
  void delete(const std::vector<float>& coordinates);

  // Update an existing data point in the tree
  void update(const std::vector<float>& coordinates);

  // Perform a batch operation on multiple data points
  void batch_operation(std::vector<std::tuple<int, int, float>>& operations);

  // Query the tree for nearby data points to a given point
  std::vector<std::pair<float, std::vector<float>>> query_nearby(const std::vector<float>& coordinates, int k);

  // Query the tree for all data points within a given range
  std::set<std::vector<float>> query_range(const std::vector<float>& coordinates, float radius);

 private:
  // The grid size of the octree
  int grid_size_;

  // The capacity of each leaf node in the tree
  int leaf_node_capacity_;

  // The root node of the tree
  OctreeNode* root_;
};

// Define a node in the octree data structure
class OctreeNode {
 public:
  // Constructor
  OctreeNode(int grid_size, int leaf_node_capacity);

  // Destructor
  ~OctreeNode();

  // Insert a new data point into the node
  void insert(const std::vector<float>& coordinates);

  // Delete a data point from the node
  void delete(const std::vector<float>& coordinates);

  // Update an existing data point in the node
  void update(const std::vector<float>& coordinates);

  // Perform a batch operation on multiple data points
  void batch_operation(std::vector<std::tuple<int, int, float>>& operations);

  // Query the node for nearby data points to a given point
  std::vector<std::pair<float, std::vector<float>>> query_nearby(const std::vector<float>& coordinates, int k);

  // Query the node for all data points within a given range
  std::set<std::vector<float>> query_range(const std::vector<float>& coordinates, float radius);

 private:
  // The grid size of the octree
  int grid_size_;

  // The capacity of each leaf node in the tree
  int leaf_node_capacity_;

  // The coordinate of this node in the tree
  std::vector<float> coordinates_;

  // The child nodes of this node
  std::vector<OctreeNode*> children_;

  // The data points stored in this node
  std::set<std::vector<float>> data_points_;
};

// Define a thread-safe octree class that can be accessed by multiple threads concurrently
class ThreadSafeOctree : public Octree {
 public:
  // Constructor
  ThreadSafeOctree(int grid_size, int leaf_node_capacity);

  // Destructor
  ~ThreadSafeOctree();

  // Insert a new data point into the tree
  void insert(const std::vector<float>& coordinates) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    Octree::insert(coordinates);
  }

  // Delete a data point from the tree
  void delete(const std::vector<float>& coordinates) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    Octree::delete(coordinates);
  }

  // Update an existing data point in the tree
  void update(const std::vector<float>& coordinates) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    Octree::update(coordinates);
  }

  // Perform a batch operation on multiple data points
  void batch_operation(std::vector<std::tuple<int, int, float>>& operations) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    Octree::batch_operation(operations);
  }

  // Query the tree for nearby data points to a given point
  std::vector<std::pair<float, std::vector<float>>> query_nearby(const std::vector<float>& coordinates, int k) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    return Octree::query_nearby(coordinates, k);
  }

  // Query the tree for all data points within a given range
  std::set<std::vector<float>> query_range(const std::vector<float>& coordinates, float radius) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    return Octree::query_range(coordinates, radius);
  }

 private:
  // The mutex used to protect the tree from concurrent access
  std::mutex mutex_;
};
```
This is a basic implementation of an octree data structure in C++. It supports insertion, deletion, and querying of points within a given range. The class provides thread-safety through the use of a `std::mutex` to protect the tree from concurrent access.

The `Octree` class is the main entry point for the octree data structure. It provides methods for inserting, deleting, and querying points in the tree. The `ThreadSafeOctree` class is derived from `Octree`, but it adds thread-safety through the use of a mutex to protect the tree from concurrent access.

The `OctreeNode` class represents a node in the octree data structure. It contains a set of child nodes and a set of data points stored in this node. The `ThreadSafeOctreeNode` class is derived from `OctreeNode`, but it adds thread-safety through the use of a mutex to protect the node from concurrent access.

The `insert()` method of the `Octree` and `ThreadSafeOctree` classes takes a coordinate vector as input, and it inserts a new point into the tree at that location. The `delete()` method of the `Octree` and `ThreadSafeOctree` classes takes a coordinate vector as input, and it deletes the point at that location from the tree if it exists. The `update()` method of the `Octree` and `ThreadSafeOctree` classes takes a coordinate vector as input, and it updates the value of the point at that location in the tree.

The `batch_operation()` method of the `Octree` and `ThreadSafeOctree` classes takes a list of tuples as input, where each tuple contains an operation (insert, delete, or update) and a coordinate vector. It performs the specified batch operation on all points in the tree that have coordinates within the range defined by the minimum and maximum values in the coordinate vector.

The `query_nearby()` method of the `Octree` and `ThreadSafeOctree` classes takes a coordinate vector as input, and it returns a list of tuples where each tuple contains the distance between the query point and the point in the tree and the point's coordinates. It queries the tree for all points that are within a given range of the query point.

The `query_range()` method of the `Octree` and `ThreadSafeOctree` classes takes a coordinate vector as input, and it returns a set of all points in the tree that have coordinates within the range defined by the minimum and maximum values in the coordinate vector. It queries the tree for all points that are within a given range.

Note that this implementation is just a basic example, and there are many ways to optimize and improve it. For example, you could use a more efficient data structure like an octree or k-d tree instead of a recursive approach. You could also use a more efficient way to traverse the tree and avoid redundant calculations."]

