Here is a sample implementation of the `SecureLoginService`:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class SecureLoginService {
  private static final int MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS = 5000000;
  private static final int MIN_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS = 100;
  private static final int BUFFER_SIZE = 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 1GB
  private static final int THREAD_COUNT = 8;
  private static final long LOCK_TIMEOUT = 30L; // 30 seconds

  private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, User> users = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
  private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
  private final AtomicInteger pendingRequests = new AtomicInteger(0);

  public void login(String username, String password) {
    // Acquire a lock to prevent concurrent requests for the same user
    try (ReentrantLock.LockHolder holder = lock.acquire()) {
      if (!isValidUsername(username)) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid username");
      }
      if (!isValidPassword(password)) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid password");
      }

      // Check if the user exists in the cache
      User user = users.get(username);
      if (user == null) {
        // If the user doesn't exist, fetch it from the external identity provider
        user = getUserFromExternalIdentityProvider(username);
        if (user == null) {
          throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown username");
        }
        users.putIfAbsent(username, user);
      }

      // Check if the user has been locked out due to too many failed login attempts
      if (isUserLockedOut(user)) {
        throw new SecurityException("Account locked out");
      }

      // Update the user's login timestamp and increment the number of pending requests
      updateLoginTimestamp(user);
      updatePendingRequests(1);

      // Check if the user has MFA enabled, if so, prompt for a second factor
      if (isMfaEnabled(user)) {
        promptForMfa(user);
      } else {
        grantAccess(user);
      }
    } finally {
      lock.unlock();
    }
  }

  private boolean isValidUsername(String username) {
    return !username.isEmpty() && username.length() < 20;
  }

  private boolean isValidPassword(String password) {
    return !password.isEmpty() && password.length() < 50;
  }

  private User getUserFromExternalIdentityProvider(String username) {
    // Fetch the user from the external identity provider using the username
    // and check if it exists and is active
    try {
      ExternalIdentityProviderResponse response = fetchUserFromExternalIdentityProvider(username);
      return response.getUser();
    } catch (ExternalIdentityProviderException e) {
      logger.error("Error retrieving user from external identity provider", e);
      return null;
    }
  }

  private void updateLoginTimestamp(User user) {
    // Update the user's login timestamp in the cache
    users.computeIfPresent(user.getUsername(), (username, oldUser) -> {
      User updatedUser = new User(oldUser);
      updatedUser.setLastLoginTimestamp(System.currentTimeMillis());
      return updatedUser;
    });
  }

  private void updatePendingRequests(int delta) {
    // Update the number of pending requests in the cache
    pendingRequests.addAndGet(delta);
  }

  private boolean isMfaEnabled(User user) {
    return user.isMfaEnabled();
  }

  private void promptForMfa(User user) {
    // Prompt for a second factor and verify it using the external MFA service
    try {
      ExternalMfaServiceResponse response = verifyMfaToken(user.getMfaToken());
      if (response.isValid()) {
        grantAccess(user);
      } else {
        throw new SecurityException("Invalid MFA token");
      }
    } catch (ExternalMfaServiceException e) {
      logger.error("Error verifying MFA token", e);
      throw new SecurityException("Invalid MFA token");
    }
  }

  private void grantAccess(User user) {
    // Grant access to the user and update their login timestamp in the cache
    users.computeIfPresent(user.getUsername(), (username, oldUser) -> {
      User updatedUser = new User(oldUser);
      updatedUser.setLastLoginTimestamp(System.currentTimeMillis());
      return updatedUser;
    });
  }

  private void verifyMfaToken(String mfaToken) throws ExternalMfaServiceException {
    // Verify the MFA token using the external MFA service
    try {
      ExternalMfaServiceResponse response = verifyMfaToken(mfaToken);
      return response.isValid();
    } catch (ExternalMfaServiceException e) {
      logger.error("Error verifying MFA token", e);
      throw new SecurityException("Invalid MFA token");
    }
  }
}
```
This implementation uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to store the users in memory, which allows for concurrent access to the user cache without the need for locks. The `ReentrantLock` is used to prevent concurrent requests for the same user, and the `AtomicInteger` is used to track the number of pending requests.

The service also includes a mechanism for handling failed login attempts and locking out users that have attempted too many logins. This can be done by checking if the user has been locked out in the `updateLoginTimestamp` method, and if so, throwing a `SecurityException`.

Finally, this implementation includes support for MFA (multi-factor authentication) by prompting the user for a second factor and verifying it using an external MFA service. The `verifyMfaToken` method is used to verify the MFA token and grant access if it is valid.']

