    char *response = malloc(256);
    memset(response, 0, 256);
    int bytes_received = recv(sock, response, 256, 0);
    if (bytes_received < 1) {
        free(response);
        return -1;
    }

    // Decrypt the DNS response using a symmetric block cipher
    char *decrypted_data = NULL;
    status = decrypt_data(response, bytes_received, &decrypted_data);
    if (status != 0) {
        free(response);
        return -1;
    }

    // Parse the DNS response and extract the IP address of the server
    struct dns_header *dns_header = NULL;
    struct dns_record *dns_record = NULL;
    status = parse_dns_response(decrypted_data, &dns_header, &dns_record);
    if (status != 0) {
        free(decrypted_data);
        return -1;
    }

    // Print the IP address of the server to the console
    printf("The IP address of %s is %s\n", hostname, inet_ntop(AF_INET, &dns_record->rdata.ipv4, buffer, 256));

    free(dns_header);
    free(dns_record);
    free(decrypted_data);
    return 0;
}
```
This code implements a simple DNS resolution library that can be used to resolve the IP address of a domain name using a TCP socket. The library is based on the `getaddrinfo` function, which allows for easy lookup of IP addresses associated with a given domain name. However, this function does not support encryption of the DNS queries or responses, which is why we need to implement our own version of the DNS resolution functionality.

The code uses the `resolve_hostname` function to resolve the IP address of a given hostname using a TCP socket. This function first checks if the given hostname has already been resolved and returns the cached result if it exists. Otherwise, it creates a new TCP socket and sends a DNS query to the server using the `send` function. It then receives the DNS response from the server using the `recv` function and decrypts it using the `decrypt_data` function. Finally, it parses the DNS response and extracts the IP address of the server using the `parse_dns_response` function.

The code also implements a simple configuration mechanism for selecting hash functions/block ciphers that are used for encryption of the DNS queries and responses. This mechanism allows us to easily switch between different cryptographic algorithms or add new ones without modifying the core functionality of the library.']

