To handle memory fragmentation, this implementation uses a technique called "buddy allocation" to allocate and free blocks of memory in a way that reduces the number of fragmented blocks. The `MemoryPool` structure maintains an array of free blocks, and when a block is freed, it is added to the array. When a new block needs to be allocated, the pool first searches for a suitable free block in the array, and if one cannot be found, it creates a new block by reallocating a larger block from the operating system.

