### Using Environment Variables

Environment variables can be used to override configuration settings at runtime. To use environment variables, simply prefix the variable name with `env_` when defining the setting in your configuration file. For example:

```ini
[http]
max_retries = env_MAX_RETRIES
retry_interval = env_RETRY_INTERVAL
```

When using environment variables, you can override the default values by setting the corresponding environment variable before starting your application. For example:

```bash
$ export MAX_RETRIES=3
$ export RETRY_INTERVAL=5
$ node app.js
```

### Using Environment Variables in Your Code

You can also use environment variables in your code by accessing the `process.env` object. For example:

```javascript
const maxRetries = process.env.MAX_RETRIES;
const retryInterval = process.env.RETRY_INTERVAL;
```

### Using External Configuration Files

You can also use external configuration files instead of the `config.json` file in your project directory. To do this, you can specify a custom path to the configuration file when starting your application. For example:

```bash
$ node app.js -c /path/to/custom/config.json
```

### Using Environment Variables with Environment Configuration Files

You can also use environment variables in conjunction with external configuration files. To do this, you can prefix the variable name with `env_` when defining the setting in your configuration file and set the corresponding environment variable before starting your application. For example:

```ini
[http]
max_retries = env_MAX_RETRIES
retry_interval = env_RETRY_INTERVAL
```

```bash
$ export MAX_RETRIES=3
$ export RETRY_INTERVAL=5
$ node app.js -c /path/to/custom/config.json
```

### Using Environment Variables with Environment Configuration Files and Overriding Defaults

You can also use environment variables in conjunction with external configuration files and override the default values by setting the corresponding environment variable before starting your application. For example:

```ini
[http]
max_retries = env_MAX_RETRIES
retry_interval = env_RETRY_INTERVAL
```

```bash
$ export MAX_RETRIES=3
$ export RETRY_INTERVAL=5
$ node app.js -c /path/to/custom/config.json
```

### Using Multiple Configuration Files

You can also use multiple configuration files to override the default values. To do this, you can specify a list of paths to the configuration files when starting your application. For example:

```bash
$ node app.js -c /path/to/config1.json -c /path/to/config2.json
```

This will load the default values from `config.json` and override them with the values from `config1.json`. Then it will load the values from `config2.json` and override them with the values from `config1.json` again.

### Using Environment Variables with Multiple Configuration Files

You can also use environment variables in conjunction with multiple configuration files to override the default values. To do this, you can prefix the variable name with `env_` when defining the setting in your configuration file and set the corresponding environment variable before starting your application. For example:

