In this example, we have a `User` model that represents a user in our system. We also have a `TokenManager` class that manages the generation and validation of tokens for authentication purposes.

The `validate()` method is used to validate input data before performing any operations on the user's account. This method checks if the email and password are valid, and if the token is valid if it was provided as a parameter.

The `invalidateToken()` method is used to invalidate a token that has been generated for a user. This method takes a token as a parameter and checks if it is a valid token, and if so, it invalidates it by removing it from the cache.

The `extendToken()` method is used to extend the expiration time of a token that has been generated for a user. This method takes a token as a parameter and checks if it is a valid token, and if so, it extends its expiration time by adding a certain amount of time to its current expiration time.

The `logout()` method is used to log the user out by invalidating their token. This method takes a token as a parameter and checks if it is a valid token, and if so, it invalidates it by removing it from the cache.

The `resetPassword()` method is used to reset a user's password. This method takes an email as a parameter and sends a password reset email to the user with a link that contains a token that is valid for 24 hours. The user can then click on this link and enter their new password in order to reset their password.

The `activateAccount()` method is used to activate a user's account after they have clicked on an activation link sent to them via email. This method takes a token as a parameter and checks if it is a valid token, and if so, it updates the user's activation status by setting it to true.

In this example, we have used Laravel's built-in validation functionality to validate input data and ensure that only valid data is stored in the database. We have also used Laravel's cache functionality to store tokens and make them invalidate after a certain amount of time has passed. This helps to prevent any malicious activities such as token replay attacks.

In the next section, we will learn how to implement authentication and authorization using these techniques in our own application."]

